{
  "case": "due2-void-keeps-obligation",
  "source": "due2-native, after the external review's R03 (docs/briefings/due2-review-2026-10-24.md): a void policy keeps its root obligations (decisions/0029)",
  "classification": "odrl-core",
  "needs": [],
  "notes": "Authored for due2; no origin result. Under odrl:invalid, a T permission and a T prohibition of one policy void its permissions and prohibitions; its root obligations stay in force (due2-workflows.md §6.2, decisions/0029). ex:deleteCopy is in force because ex:use matches the request, constraints aside (§6.1), and it is violated because 2026-06-01 has passed with no witness (§5.3). Scenario 1 is the research request, with no conflict: the violated obligation denies. Scenario 2 is the marketing request, which voids the pair: the obligation still denies, so asking for the forbidden use does not escape it. Under the reading that a void policy keeps nothing, scenario 2 would be NotApplicable while scenario 1 is Deny, and adding ex:noMarketing would have raised the verdict, against L5. Scenarios 3 and 4 show that the Deny comes from the obligation: under violations = ignore, or with the copy deleted in time, the void policy gives NotApplicable. The bearer is ex:Steward, the obligation's assignee.",
  "prefixes": {
    "ex": "https://example.org/"
  },
